\section{What \code{bias} is, and why it does not port}{

\code{bias} is the indecision point: a constant added to the utility
difference, so a subject with \code{bias} above zero prefers option 1 at
equal value. It has an identity link.

\strong{It is not hBayesDM's \code{alpha} renamed, and there is no fixed
transform between them.} hBayesDM's \code{prl_fictitious} writes the
choice probability as \code{inv_logit(beta * (alpha - (ev1 - ev2)))},
which is \code{inv_logit(beta * (ev1 - ev2) - beta * alpha)} with the
option labels swapped. This family writes
\code{inv_logit(tau * (ev1 - ev2) + bias)}. Matching the two term by term
gives

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{bias = -tau * alpha_hBayesDM
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The factor is \code{tau}, which is ESTIMATED, so the map depends on the
fit and no constant relates the two parameters. At the \code{tau = 3} this
page's example uses, an indecision point carried across unchanged is
wrong by a factor of three, and the resulting model converges quietly.
Convert through the equation above, using the same fit's \code{tau}, or
refit.

What is implemented here is the equation printed at the top of this
page, and it is that equation the Stan identity in
\code{tests/testthat/test-stan-identity.R} checks to 8.5e-14. The relation
above is read off hBayesDM 2.0.0's published Stan source rather than
measured against a fit: \code{dev/hbayesdm-crosscheck.R} would measure it
and has not been run, for the toolchain reason recorded in its
